{"title":"Inradius","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>inradius</b> of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polygon\" title=\"Polygon\">polygon</a> is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Radius\" title=\"Radius\">radius</a> of its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Incircle\" title=\"Incircle\">incircle</a> (assuming an incircle exists). It is commonly denoted <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/d/3ad1b30f1350fc22f90ce7a79f2567025d4fe4c0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pathpen = linewidth(0.7); pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(1.5,2),I=incenter(A,B,C),F=foot(I,A,B); D(MP(&quot;A&quot;,A)--MP(&quot;B&quot;,B)--MP(&quot;C&quot;,C,N)--cycle); D(CR(D(MP(&quot;I&quot;,I,SW)),inradius(A,B,C))); D(F--I--foot(I,B,C)--I--foot(I,C,A)); D(rightanglemark(I,F,B,5)); MP(&quot;r&quot;,(F+I)/2,E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"158\" /></center>\n<p>In a triangle, the incenter is where the three angle bisectors meet.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"A_Property\">A Property</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: A Property\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> has inradius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Semi-perimeter\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Semi-perimeter\">semi-perimeter</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, then the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Area\" title=\"Area\">area</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/8/c889d12451d321614e219737373586e45b71a141.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$rs$\" width=\"16\" height=\"8\" />. This formula holds true for other polygons if the incircle exists.</li></ul>\n<h1><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h1>\n<p>Add in the incircle and drop the altitudes from the incenter to the sides of the triangle. Also draw the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/2/6a2572af46396706dc24e0b72b7ec40cb0a8caee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AI}, \\overline{BI}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"55\" height=\"19\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/4/4d4012a66b317203f3faac588fbc458b72a99de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CI}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" />. After this, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/7/1878dcdec1c4dd405a85f22995487ce46741c7ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB},\\overline{AC},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> are the bases of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/d/c3d73592363303a325ea9756896f8a7109f35140.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{AIB}, {AIC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"99\" height=\"17\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/5/c65d432527a52254fb6ca7785fc38101a192b7c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${BIC}$\" width=\"38\" height=\"12\" /> respectively. But they all have the same height (the inradius), so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d3ee094c6488782f9e99eaa929c577415f0a4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABC]=\\frac{r(a+b+c)}{2} =rs$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"217\" height=\"40\" />.\n</p><p>Also the inradius of a incircle inscribed in a right triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/e/73ed6745475287846a3a44c45cd06921f2c8c9aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a+b-c}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"72\" height=\"37\" /> as by drawing three inradii to the three tangent points, then A to that tangent point is equal to A to the other tangent point (explained in circles) and etc for B and C. After doing it for B and C, C (the hypotenuse) should equal the equivalent tangent of A and the equivalent tangent of B added together, thus our equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/1/1e10db7df3e7fccf99a1ac6de439f3e2cf0723fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=rs$\" width=\"54\" height=\"13\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problems\">Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>Verify the inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/d/11dc030b157e474cc9cd7eca9b9c05fee5e2e7e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R \\ge 2r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" />. (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumcenter\" title=\"Circumcenter\">Circumcenter</a> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">Inradius</a>)</li>\n<li>Verify the identity <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/b/45b148e0d20ba554ba8aca454e543d3b48c37f19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{A}+\\cos{B}+\\cos{C}=\\frac{r+R}{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"240\" height=\"37\" /> (see <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Carnot%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Carnot&#39;s Theorem\">Carnot's Theorem</a>).</li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 15\">2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 15</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018091401\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.022 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.046 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 106/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3825/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3235:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018091401 and revision id 286310. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}